</figure>

<p>
As an example, <xref ref="fig-3d-point-example"/> shows the point
<m>(-4, 3, -5)</m>. Because the first coordinate is negative we move in
the direction opposite the positive <m>x</m>-axis, and because the third
coordinate is negative the point lies below the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-point-example">
<caption>
The point <m>(-4,3,-5)</m>. The box drawn with dashed edges shows how the
three coordinates locate the point relative to the origin.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
The coordinate axes with a rectangular box whose top back corner is at the
origin. Edges of length 4 along the negative x direction, 3 along the
positive y direction, and 5 straight down locate the point with
coordinates negative 4, 3, negative 5 at the far bottom corner of the box.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.72, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (-5.6,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1.2,0) -- (0,5.2,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-6.6) -- (0,0,3.0) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above right] at (0,0,0) {$0$};
% the three magenta legs
\draw[magenta, very thick] (0,0,0) -- (-4,0,0) node[midway, above] {$-4$};
\draw[magenta, very thick] (-4,0,0) -- (-4,3,0) node[midway, above] {$3$};
\draw[magenta, very thick] (-4,3,0) -- (-4,3,-5) node[midway, right] {$-5$};
% box edges (dashed)
\draw[dashed, gray] (0,0,0) -- (0,3,0) -- (-4,3,0);
\draw[dashed, gray] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,-5) -- (-4,0,-5) -- (-4,0,0);
\draw[dashed, gray] (0,0,-5) -- (0,3,-5) -- (-4,3,-5);
\draw[dashed, gray] (0,3,0) -- (0,3,-5);
\draw[dashed, gray] (-4,0,-5) -- (-4,3,-5);
\fill[magenta] (-4,3,-5) circle (2pt);
\node[right] at (-4,3,-5) {$(-4,3,-5)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-3d-planes-octants">
<title>Coordinate Planes and Octants</title>

<p>
The three coordinate axes determine three <term>coordinate planes</term>.
The <m>xy</m>-plane is the plane containing the <m>x</m>- and
<m>y</m>-axes, and it is described by the single equation <m>z = 0</m>;
similarly the <m>xz</m>-plane is <m>y = 0</m> and the <m>yz</m>-plane is
<m>x = 0</m>. These three planes are shown in
<xref ref="fig-3d-coordinate-planes"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-coordinate-planes">
<caption>
The three coordinate planes: the <m>xy</m>-plane <m>(z=0)</m>, the
<m>xz</m>-plane <m>(y=0)</m>, and the <m>yz</m>-plane <m>(x=0)</m>. They
divide space into eight octants.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Three shaded rectangles meeting at the origin at right angles represent
the three coordinate planes. The horizontal one is the x y plane where z
equals zero, and the two vertical ones are the x z plane where y equals
zero and the y z plane where x equals zero.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.95, >=stealth]
% xy-plane (z = 0)
\fill[orange!25, opacity=0.75] (-2.6,-2.6,0) -- (2.6,-2.6,0) --
                               (2.6,2.6,0) -- (-2.6,2.6,0) -- cycle;
% xz-plane (y = 0)
\fill[orange!45, opacity=0.65] (-2.6,0,-2.6) -- (2.6,0,-2.6) --
                               (2.6,0,2.6) -- (-2.6,0,2.6) -- cycle;
% yz-plane (x = 0)
\fill[orange!35, opacity=0.65] (0,-2.6,-2.6) -- (0,2.6,-2.6) --
                               (0,2.6,2.6) -- (0,-2.6,2.6) -- cycle;
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,3.4) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above left] at (0,0,0) {$O$};
\node[align=center] at (-1.5,-1.7,1.9) {$xz$-plane\\ $(y=0)$};
\node[align=center] at (0,1.9,1.9) {$yz$-plane\\ $(x=0)$};
\node[align=center] at (1.6,1.5,0) {$xy$-plane\\ $(z=0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
In the plane the two axes create four quadrants; in space the three
coordinate planes create eight <term>octants</term>. The
<term>first octant</term> is the one in which <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and
<m>z</m> are all positive.
</p>

<p>
More generally, equations such as <m>x = a</m>, <m>y = b</m>, and
<m>z = c</m> represent planes parallel to the <m>yz</m>-,
<m>xz</m>-, and <m>xy</m>-planes respectively. Each of these equations
places one restriction on a point of space and leaves the other two
coordinates free, so each describes a plane. Consequently a point can be
thought of as the intersection of three such planes: the point
<m>P(1,1,1)</m> of <xref ref="fig-3d-three-planes"/> is the intersection
of the planes <m>x = 1</m>, <m>y = 1</m>, and <m>z = 1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-three-planes">
<caption>
A point as the intersection of three planes. The planes <m>x=1</m>,
<m>y=1</m>, and <m>z=1</m> meet pairwise in three lines, and all three
lines meet in the single point <m>P(1,1,1)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Three shaded planes, one for x equals 1, one for y equals 1, and one for z
equals 1, intersect one another at right angles. Each pair of planes meets
in a red line: the planes x equals 1 and y equals 1 meet in a vertical
line, the planes x equals 1 and z equals 1 meet in a line running in the y
direction, and the planes y equals 1 and z equals 1 meet in a line running
in the x direction. All three red lines cross at the single common point,
marked in green and labelled P with coordinates 1, 1, 1.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=1.15, >=stealth]
% plane z = 1 (horizontal, pink)
\fill[purple!22, opacity=0.85] (-1.7,-1.7,1) -- (1.7,-1.7,1) --
                               (1.7,1.7,1) -- (-1.7,1.7,1) -- cycle;
% plane y = 1 (gray)
\fill[gray!30, opacity=0.7] (-1.7,1,-1.7) -- (1.7,1,-1.7) --
                            (1.7,1,1.7) -- (-1.7,1,1.7) -- cycle;
% plane x = 1 (blue)
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.7] (1,-1.7,-1.7) -- (1,1.7,-1.7) --
                            (1,1.7,1.7) -- (1,-1.7,1.7) -- cycle;
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.5,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.3) node[above] {$z$};
% lines of intersection of each pair of planes
\draw[red!70!black, thick] (1,1,-1.7) -- (1,1,1.7);
\draw[red!70!black, thick] (1,-1.7,1) -- (1,1.7,1);
\draw[red!70!black, thick] (-1.7,1,1) -- (1.7,1,1);
\fill[green!50!black] (1,1,1) circle (2pt);
\node[green!50!black, above right] at (1,1,1) {$P(1,1,1)$};
\node[purple!70!black] at (-1.5,-1.2,1) {$z=1$};
\node[gray!50!black] at (-1.5,1,-1.4) {$y=1$};
\node[cyan!60!black] at (1,1.5,-1.4) {$x=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-3d-distance">
<title>Distance Between Two Points</title>

<p>
The distance between two points <m>P_1(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> and
<m>P_2(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m> is denoted by <m>\left\|P_1P_2\right\|</m> and can
be computed with the Pythagorean theorem. As
<xref ref="fig-3d-distance"/> shows, the segment <m>P_1P_2</m> is the
diagonal of a rectangular box whose edges are parallel to the coordinate
axes, and applying the Pythagorean theorem twice gives
<md xml:id="eq-3d-distance" number="yes">
\left\|P_1P_2\right\| =
\sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2 + (y_1-y_2)^2 + (z_1-z_2)^2}.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-distance">
<caption>
The distance between <m>P_1(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> and
<m>P_2(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m> is the length of the diagonal of a box with edges
parallel to the axes. The helper corners are <m>A(x_2,y_1,z_1)</m> and
<m>B(x_2,y_2,z_1)</m>; applying the Pythagorean theorem first in the
triangle <m>P_1AB</m>, with its right angle at <m>A</m>, and then in the
vertical triangle <m>P_1BP_2</m>, with its right angle at <m>B</m>, gives
<xref ref="eq-3d-distance"/>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A rectangular box with edges parallel to the coordinate axes. The point P
one sits at the near lower corner and P two at the opposite upper corner,
joined by a violet diagonal. Two intermediate corners are marked: A,
reached from P one along a blue edge of length the absolute value of x two
minus x one, and B, reached from A along an orange edge of length the
absolute value of y two minus y one. A green vertical edge of length the
absolute value of z two minus z one rises from B to P two. A red segment
joins P one to B across the base, and small squares mark the right angles
at A and at B.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
% Orientation, colors and labels deliberately match the video in
% fig-video-distance-formula: x to the right, y back, z up; the edge
% lengths are blue, orange and green, the base diagonal P1B is red and
% the main diagonal P1P2 is violet.
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(0.98cm,-0.16cm)}, y={(0.45cm,0.56cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=1.05, >=stealth,
                    edge/.style={line width=1.6pt},
                    lbl/.style={font=\small}]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (5.4,0,0) node[below right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.6,0) node[above left] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.2) node[left] {$z$};
% box corners; the box is set out along x so the axes stay clear of it
\coordinate (P1) at (1.6,0.5,0.9);
\coordinate (A)  at (4.0,0.5,0.9);
\coordinate (B)  at (4.0,2.6,0.9);
\coordinate (P2) at (4.0,2.6,2.9);
% box, drawn first so the colored edges sit on top of it
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin]
  (1.6,0.5,0.9) -- (4.0,0.5,0.9) -- (4.0,2.6,0.9) -- (1.6,2.6,0.9) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin]
  (1.6,0.5,2.9) -- (4.0,0.5,2.9) -- (4.0,2.6,2.9) -- (1.6,2.6,2.9) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (1.6,0.5,0.9) -- (1.6,0.5,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (4.0,0.5,0.9) -- (4.0,0.5,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (4.0,2.6,0.9) -- (4.0,2.6,2.9);
\draw[cyan!65!blue, thin] (1.6,2.6,0.9) -- (1.6,2.6,2.9);
% the three edge lengths
\draw[edge, blue!70!cyan] (P1) -- (A)
  node[lbl, blue!70!cyan, pos=0.68, above=2pt] {$|x_2-x_1|$};
\draw[edge, orange!90!black] (A) -- (B)
  node[lbl, orange!90!black, midway, right=1pt] {$|y_2-y_1|$};
\draw[edge, green!60!black] (B) -- (P2)
  node[lbl, green!60!black, midway, right=1pt] {$|z_2-z_1|$};
% base diagonal and full diagonal
\draw[edge, red!80!black] (P1) -- (B);
\draw[edge, violet] (P1) -- (P2);
% right angles at A (in the base) and at B (vertical)
\draw[yellow!65!black, line width=0.9pt]
  (3.72,0.5,0.9) -- (3.72,0.72,0.9) -- (4.0,0.72,0.9);
\draw[yellow!65!black, line width=0.9pt]
  (3.737,2.37,0.9) -- (3.737,2.37,1.2) -- (4.0,2.6,1.2);
% corners
\fill[red] (P1) circle (2.2pt);
\fill[red] (P2) circle (2.2pt);
\fill[black] (A) circle (2.0pt);
\fill[black] (B) circle (2.0pt);
\node[lbl, red, below left, xshift=16pt, yshift=-5pt] at (P1) {$P_1(x_1,y_1,z_1)$};
\node[lbl, red, above right]  at (P2) {$P_2(x_2,y_2,z_2)$};
\node[lbl, below right] at (A) {$A(x_2,y_1,z_1)$};
\node[lbl, right=2pt]   at (B) {$B(x_2,y_2,z_1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The video in <xref ref="fig-video-distance-formula"/> builds this box one
edge at a time and applies the Pythagorean theorem twice, in the same two
steps and with the same colors as <xref ref="fig-3d-distance"/>. Step 1
works in the base, where the right angle at <m>A</m> gives
<m>\left\|P_1B\right\|^2 = \left|x_2-x_1\right|^2 +
\left|y_2-y_1\right|^2</m>; step 2 goes up the vertical edge, where the
right angle at <m>B</m> gives <m>\left\|P_1P_2\right\|^2 =
\left\|P_1B\right\|^2 + \left|z_2-z_1\right|^2</m>. Substituting the first
equation into the second gives <xref ref="eq-3d-distance"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-video-distance-formula">
<caption>
Deriving the distance formula in space by applying the Pythagorean
theorem twice: first in the base triangle <m>P_1AB</m>, then in the
vertical triangle <m>P_1BP_2</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-distance-formula"
       source="videos/DistanceFormulaProof.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/DistanceFormulaProof.jpg" width="85%"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-3d-spheres">
<title>Spheres</title>

<p>
A <term>sphere</term> of radius <m>a</m> is the set of all points
<m>P(x,y,z)</m> whose distance from a fixed center
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m> equals <m>a</m>. Writing that condition with the
distance formula <xref ref="eq-3d-distance"/> and squaring both sides
gives the equation of the sphere,
<md xml:id="eq-3d-sphere" number="yes">
(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = a^2.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-sphere">
<caption>
A sphere of radius <m>a</m> centered at
<m>P_0 = P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m>: the set of all points <m>P(x,y,z)</m> at
distance <m>a</m> from the center.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded sphere with its center marked and a segment of length a drawn
from the center to a point P on the surface, illustrating that every
point of the sphere is the same distance a from the center.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
% drawn entirely in the picture plane so the ball, its centre and the
% radius are guaranteed to line up
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.9) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (3.6,-0.95) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.3,-1.05) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above left] at (0,0) {$0$};
% the sphere
\shade[ball color=orange!30, opacity=0.92] (1.35,0.95) circle (1.05cm);
% equator, to help read it as a solid
\draw[orange!55!black, dashed, opacity=0.8]
  (1.35,0.95) ellipse (1.05cm and 0.3cm);
% centre and one radius
\fill[magenta] (1.35,0.95) circle (1.8pt);
\draw[magenta, very thick, dashed] (1.35,0.95) -- (2.09,1.69);
\fill[magenta] (2.09,1.69) circle (1.8pt);
\node[magenta] at (1.58,1.44) {$a$};
\node[magenta, above right=1pt] at (2.09,1.69) {$P(x,y,z)$};
% the centre is labelled briefly and pulled clear of the ball with a
% leader; its coordinates are given in the caption
\draw[gray, thin] (1.35,0.95) -- (0.45,0.12);
\node[magenta, below left=0pt] at (0.45,0.12) {$P_0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-3d-complete-square">
<title>Recognizing a Sphere by Completing the Square</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the geometric surface represented by the following equation.
<md>
x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2x + 4y = 0
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by completing the squares in <m>x</m> and in <m>y</m>:
<md>
<mrow>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2x + 4y \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>(x^2 + 2x + 1) - 1 + (y^2 + 4y + 4) - 4 + z^2 \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>(x+1)^2 + (y+2)^2 + z^2 \amp = 5.</mrow>
</md>
Comparing with <xref ref="eq-3d-sphere"/>, this is the equation of a
sphere with center <m>P_0 = (-1,-2,0)</m> and radius
<m>a = \sqrt 5</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-3d-describing-regions">
<title>Describing Regions in Space</title>

<p>
A single equation in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m> normally describes
a surface, two simultaneous equations describe the curve where two
surfaces meet, and inequalities describe solid regions. The next example
collects several of these situations.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-3d-describe">
<title>Describing Equations and Inequalities Geometrically</title>
<statement>
<p>
Describe the geometrical meaning of the following equalities and
inequalities.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>x = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x = 1,\; y = 2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>1 \le x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4,\; z \le 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 1,\; z = 3</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 1,\; -1 \le z \le 1</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> This is the equation of the set of all points whose
<m>x</m>-coordinate equals <m>1</m>, that is
<m>\{(x,y,z) \in \R^3 : x = 1\}</m>. Geometrically it is a plane parallel
to the <m>yz</m>-plane, shown in <xref ref="fig-3d-plane-x1"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-plane-x1">
<caption>
Part <alert>A</alert>: the plane <m>x=1</m>, parallel to the
<m>yz</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded rectangle parallel to the y z plane, offset from it in the
positive x direction, representing the plane x equals 1.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.75] (1,-1.8,-1.8) -- (1,1.8,-1.8) -- (1,1.8,1.8) -- (1,-1.8,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black] (1,-1.8,-1.8) -- (1,1.8,-1.8) -- (1,1.8,1.8) -- (1,-1.8,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (-2.2,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.4,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.4,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-2.4) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.4) node[above] {$z$};
\node[cyan!70!black] at (1,1.4,1.6) {$x=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>B.</alert> This is the intersection of the two planes <m>x = 1</m>
and <m>y = 2</m>, which is a line. The line is parallel to the
<m>z</m>-axis, since <m>z</m> is unrestricted, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-3d-line-x1y2"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-line-x1y2">
<caption>
Part <alert>B</alert>: the planes <m>x=1</m> and <m>y=2</m> meet in a
line parallel to the <m>z</m>-axis, highlighted in red.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
<p>
Two shaded planes, one for x equals 1 and one for y equals 2, crossing
each other. Their intersection is a vertical red line parallel to the z
axis.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, y={(0.87cm,-0.5cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05, >=stealth]
\fill[cyan!25, opacity=0.6] (1,-1,-1.8) -- (1,3,-1.8) -- (1,3,1.8) -- (1,-1,1.8) -- cycle;
\fill[orange!25, opacity=0.6] (-1,2,-1.8) -- (3,2,-1.8) -- (3,2,1.8) -- (-1,2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black] (1,-1,-1.8) -- (1,3,-1.8) -- (1,3,1.8) -- (1,-1,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[orange!70!black] (-1,2,-1.8) -- (3,2,-1.8) -- (3,2,1.8) -- (-1,2,1.8) -- cycle;
\draw[thick] (-2.2,0,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[thick] (0,-2.2,0) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.4,0) node[below right] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0,-2.2) -- (0,0,0);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.2) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[red!70!black, very thick] (1,2,-1.8) -- (1,2,1.8);
\node[cyan!70!black] at (1,-0.7,1.6) {$x=1$};
\node[orange!80!black] at (2.7,2,1.6) {$y=2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>C.</alert> By <xref ref="eq-3d-sphere"/> this is a sphere of
radius one centered at the origin <m>(0,0,0)</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-3d-sphere-unit"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-sphere-unit">
<caption>
Part <alert>C</alert>: the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>, of radius one
centered at the origin.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<description>
<p>
A shaded sphere of radius one centered at the origin, with a dashed
ellipse marking its equator.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.8,-0.75) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.0,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\shade[ball color=cyan!30, opacity=0.92] (0,0) circle (1.3cm);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed, opacity=0.8] (0,0) ellipse (1.3cm and 0.36cm);
\node[below] at (0,-1.9) {$x^2+y^2+z^2=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>D.</alert> You can think of <m>1 \le x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 4</m> as
the set of all points whose distance from the origin is at least
<m>1</m> and at most <m>2</m>. Therefore it represents the region trapped
between the two spheres centered at the origin with radii <m>r = 1</m>
and <m>r = 2</m>. Then there is the extra constraint <m>z \le 0</m>,
which restricts us to the region below the <m>xy</m>-plane. The result,
shown in <xref ref="fig-3d-shell"/>, is a half of a thick spherical
shell.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-shell">
<caption>
Part <alert>D</alert>: the region between the spheres of radius <m>1</m>
and <m>2</m>, cut by the condition <m>z \le 0</m> to leave only the half
below the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A hemispherical shell sitting below the x y plane. Its outer surface is
the sphere of radius two and its inner surface, visible as a smaller
hemisphere hollowed out of it, is the sphere of radius one.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% drawn directly in the picture plane: the region 1 <= r <= 2 is the
% solution, and r = 1 is its inner boundary surface, so both are
% filled blue; only the true cavity r < 1 stays blank
\begin{scope}
  % outer hemisphere (radius 2): dome curve combined with its full rim
  % ellipse, so the flat lid at z = 0 is colored too, not just the
  % curved dome underneath it
  \fill[cyan!30, opacity=0.9]
    (-2,0) arc (180:360:2cm and 0.55cm) arc (0:-180:2cm)
    (0,0) ellipse (2cm and 0.55cm);
  % inner hemisphere (radius 1): its curved rim is part of the
  % solution's boundary, filled the same blue as the outer surface;
  % its flat top disk is lighter, to show the cavity beneath it is
  % not included
  \fill[cyan!30] (-1,0) arc (180:360:1cm and 0.28cm) arc (0:-180:1cm);
  \fill[cyan!12, opacity=0.9] (0,0) ellipse (1cm and 0.28cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2,0) arc (180:360:2cm and 0.55cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2,0) arc (180:0:2cm and 0.55cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-2,0) arc (180:360:2cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1,0) arc (180:360:1cm and 0.28cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1,0) arc (180:0:1cm and 0.28cm);
  \draw[cyan!55!black] (-1,0) arc (180:360:1cm);
\end{scope}
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.9,-0.75) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.6,-1.15) node[below left] {$x$};
\node[above right] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node at (0.62,-0.42) {$1$};
\node at (1.5,-0.95) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>E.</alert> In the plane, <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> is a circle. In space
the variable <m>z</m> is unrestricted, so the equation represents an
infinitely long cylinder of radius one whose axis is the <m>z</m>-axis,
as in <xref ref="fig-3d-cylinder-infinite"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-cylinder-infinite">
<caption>
Part <alert>E</alert>: the infinite cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. The
dashed lines indicate that it extends without bound in both directions
along the <m>z</m>-axis.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<description>
<p>
A cylinder of radius one centered on the z axis, with dashed lines
extending past its top and bottom rims to indicate it continues
indefinitely in both directions.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$z$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.3,-0.65) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.0,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
\fill[cyan!22, opacity=0.85] (-1.3,-1.6) -- (-1.3,1.6) arc (180:360:1.3cm and 0.36cm) -- (1.3,-1.6) arc (360:180:1.3cm and 0.36cm);
\fill[cyan!10, opacity=0.9] (0,1.6) ellipse (1.3cm and 0.36cm);
\draw[cyan!55!black] (-1.3,-1.6) -- (-1.3,1.6);
\draw[cyan!55!black] (1.3,-1.6) -- (1.3,1.6);
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (0,1.6) ellipse (1.3cm and 0.36cm);
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (0,-1.6) ellipse (1.3cm and 0.36cm);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (-1.3,1.6) -- (-1.3,2.2);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (1.3,1.6) -- (1.3,2.2);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (-1.3,-1.6) -- (-1.3,-2.2);
\draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (1.3,-1.6) -- (1.3,-2.2);
\node[below] at (0,-2.6) {$x^2+y^2=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>F.</alert> This is the intersection of the plane <m>z = 3</m> and
the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, which is a circle of radius one lying in
the plane <m>z = 3</m> and centered at <m>(0,0,3)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<alert>G.</alert> The difference between this part and part
<alert>E</alert> is that <m>z \in [-1,1]</m>, which means the cylinder is
cut down to a piece of height two.
<xref ref="fig-3d-cylinders"/> shows the surfaces of parts
<alert>F</alert> and <alert>G</alert> together.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-3d-cylinders">
<caption>
Parts <alert>F</alert> and <alert>G</alert>. On the left, the finite
cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> with <m>-1 \le z \le 1</m>. On the right, the
same cylinder extended, cut by the plane <m>z = 3</m>; the solution to
part <alert>F</alert>, the circle where they meet, is highlighted in
red.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two pictures side by side. On the left a short cylinder of radius one
standing on the z axis between z equals negative one and z equals one. On
the right a taller cylinder crossed by a horizontal plane at height three,
meeting it in a circle.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85, >=stealth]
% ---------------- left: finite cylinder, -1 <= z <= 1 ----------------
\begin{scope}[xshift=-3.4cm]
  \draw[->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.5,-0.7) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.1,-0.95) node[below left] {$x$};
  % cylinder body between z = -1 and z = 1: filled out to the near
  % (front) rim curves rather than a flat rectangle, so it reads as a
  % curved tube surface rather than a plane
  \fill[purple!22, opacity=0.85]
    (-1.4,-1) -- (-1.4,1) arc (180:360:1.4cm and 0.4cm)
    -- (1.4,-1) arc (360:180:1.4cm and 0.4cm);
  % inside back wall, visible through the open top rim: fills the top
  % ellipse's disk exactly, sharing the front arc as its boundary with
  % the body fill above, so the two meet with no gap or overlap
  \fill[purple!22, opacity=0.85] (0,1) ellipse (1.4cm and 0.4cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black] (-1.4,-1) -- (-1.4,1);
  \draw[purple!60!black] (1.4,-1) -- (1.4,1);
  % top and bottom rims: outlines only, since x^2+y^2=1 is just the
  % tube's lateral surface and has no top or bottom cap
  \draw[purple!60!black, thick] (0,1) ellipse (1.4cm and 0.4cm);
  \draw[purple!60!black, thick] (0,-1) ellipse (1.4cm and 0.4cm);
  \node[right] at (1.45,1.05) {$z=1$};
  \node[right] at (1.45,-1.05) {$z=-1$};
  \node[below] at (0,-2.5) {$x^2+y^2=1,\;-1\le z\le 1$};
\end{scope}
% ---------------- right: cylinder cut by the plane z = 3 -------------
\begin{scope}[xshift=3.4cm]
  \draw[->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2.5,-0.7) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (-2.1,-0.95) node[below left] {$x$};
  % tall cylinder and plane: outlines only, uncolored, since they are
  % only context for part F, whose actual solution is the circle
  % where they meet, highlighted in red below
  \draw[blue!55!black] (-1.2,-1.8) -- (-1.2,2.6);
  \draw[blue!55!black] (1.2,-1.8) -- (1.2,2.6);
  \draw[blue!55!black, thick] (0,2.6) ellipse (1.2cm and 0.34cm);
  \draw[blue!55!black, thick] (0,-1.8) ellipse (1.2cm and 0.34cm);
  \draw[red!45!black] (-2.6,1.1) -- (2.6,0.8) -- (2.6,1.6) -- (-2.6,1.9) -- cycle;
  % the circle of intersection: the actual solution to part F
  \draw[red!60!black, very thick] (0,1.4) ellipse (1.2cm and 0.34cm);
  \node[red!60!black, right] at (2.6,0.95) {$z=3$};
  \node[below] at (0,-2.5) {$x^2+y^2=1,\; z=3$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
